---
/**
 * The playground, one static page per curated sample.
 *
 * WHY A ROUTE PER SAMPLE RATHER THAN A PICKER THAT SWAPS IN THE BROWSER. Each
 * page carries exactly one program, one recorded colouring and one recording.
 * The alternative was one page holding all thirteen, which would have meant
 * shipping every listing to every visitor so that a control could reveal one of
 * them. A site arguing that a program should link only what it uses does not get
 * to do that, and there is a second reason that matters more: a lesson deep
 * links a sample, and a real URL resolves before any script runs, so the link
 * works with scripts off and survives being bookmarked or shared.
 *
 * `/playground/` and `/playground/hello/` are both generated. The bare path is
 * the entry point the masthead links, and it shows the first sample in the
 * recording, so there is no such thing as an empty playground.
 */
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Playground from "../../components/Playground.astro";
import { activeEngine } from "../../playground/registry";
import { CAPABILITY_LABEL } from "../../playground/types";
import type { Capability } from "../../playground/types";
import type { CuratedSample } from "../../playground/samples";
import { curatedSamples, wasmProvenance } from "../../playground/samples";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  /* The bare route, plus one per recorded sample. The recording decides what
   * exists, so a sample that failed to record has no page rather than a page
   * that cannot run. */
  return [
    { params: { sample: undefined }, props: { sample: curatedSamples[0] } },
    ...curatedSamples.map((sample) => ({
      params: { sample: sample.id },
      props: { sample },
    })),
  ];
}

interface Props {
  sample: CuratedSample;
}

const { sample } = Astro.props as Props;

const engine = activeEngine();
const caps = engine.capabilities();
const missing = (["compile", "emit-iyimod", "mod-dump", "format"] as Capability[])
  .filter((cap) => !caps.supported.includes(cap))
  .map((cap) => CAPABILITY_LABEL[cap]);
---

<Page
  title={`Playground: ${sample.id} | iyi`}
  summary={`The iyi playground runs ${sample.path}, cross-compiled to wasm32-wasi by the compiler and executed on this page against a WASI host written in TypeScript. It does not compile, and it says so.`}
>
  <section class="spread">
    <div class="to-wide intro">
      <h1>playground</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The samples on this page really run. Each one was cross-compiled to
        <code>wasm32-wasi</code> by the iyi compiler, linked, and committed to
        this repository with its digest and the machine that produced it. The
        page fetches the module, refuses it if the digest does not match the
        record, and executes it against a WASI preview1 host written in
        TypeScript, because a browser has no <code>node:wasi</code>. What it
        prints is what the program printed.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it does not do is compile. There is no iyi compiler in a browser,
        so the pane below is the recorded program and editing it cannot change
        what runs. The page says so on the control, in a line under it, and by
        withdrawing the recorded colouring the moment the text stops being the
        recorded text. {
          missing.length > 0 && (
            <Fragment>
              For the same reason the {missing.join(", ")} controls are disabled
              rather than hidden, each one naming the capability the engine does
              not claim.
            </Fragment>
          )
        }
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Playground sample={sample} />

  <section class="spread">
    <div class="to-wide why">
      <hr class="rule" />
      <h2>What is real here, precisely</h2>
      <p>
        Worth being exact, because "runs in your browser" is a claim a lot of
        pages make loosely.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The module is real compiler output.</strong> It came from
          <code>iyi build --cross-compile --target wasm32-wasi</code> on the
          machine stamped above, then a link step, because for this target the
          fork prints a <code>cc --target=wasm32-wasi</code> command rather than
          calling <code>wasm-ld</code>. Both are recorded in
          <code>site/records/wasm/manifest.json</code>, and
          <code>site/scripts/records.mjs</code> fails the build if a module's
          digest does not match the bytes on disk.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The execution is real.</strong> The host implements the WASI
          preview1 calls the modules import, and it grants what a page can
          honestly grant: standard output and standard error captured per write,
          standard input open and at end of file, an empty environment, real
          clocks, real randomness, and no filesystem at all. A call it does not
          implement returns an errno rather than trapping, so a program can see
          the failure instead of dying.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>There are no threads, and there cannot be.</strong> GitHub
          Pages cannot set HTTP response headers, so
          <code>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy</code> and
          <code>Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy</code> cannot be sent, so this
          document is never cross-origin isolated, so
          <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> does not exist here. That rules out wasm
          threads and it rules out synchronous standard input through
          <code>Atomics.wait</code>. It is also why output arrives in the chunks
          the program wrote but only once <code>_start</code> returns: a wasm
          call in this page cannot be suspended.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The type checking is not here, and that is not laziness.</strong>
          The compiler reports an error by raising, and <code>raise</code> on
          <code>wasm32</code> does not unwind, so a compiler compiled for this
          page could not hand a diagnostic back to whatever called it. It could
          only die. The full investigation is on branch
          <code>docs/playground-feasibility</code> at
          <code>doc/website/PLAYGROUND-FEASIBILITY.md</code>. So the diagnostics
          above are the real compiler's output on real broken programs, captured
          at build time, and the page says which of the two it is rather than
          leaving a reader to assume.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One sample fails on purpose.</strong> The recording marks it,
          the picker marks it, and the card carries the sentence. It is in the
          set because a target that cannot open a file is a thing worth knowing
          about the target, and hiding it would make the other twelve less
          believable.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The interface underneath all of this assumes engines that do part of the
        job. <code>capabilities()</code> is the only input to how the page is
        shaped, so an engine that could check source but not link would get a
        larger playground here without a markup change, and one that could do
        neither would get a smaller one. To swap the engine, follow the three
        steps in <code>src/playground/registry.ts</code>: add an implementation,
        register it there, and report only what it actually does.
      </p>
      <aside class="evidence">
        <dl>
          <dt>compiler</dt>
          <dd>{wasmProvenance.compiler}</dd>
          <dt>recorded at</dt>
          <dd>{wasmProvenance.commit}</dd>
          <dt>regenerated by</dt>
          <dd>{wasmProvenance.command}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin-block: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .intro h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    margin-block-end: 0.6rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: var(--lead-snug);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .intro p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .why {
    margin-block: 1rem 0;
  }

  .why h2 {
    margin-block: 1.8rem 0.9rem;
  }

  .why p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .why ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    margin-block: 0 1.05em;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .why li {
    margin-block: 0.55em;
  }
</style>
